# REV(1)

## NAME

rev - reverse lines of a file

## SYNOPSIS

| **rev** [ file ] ...

## DESCRIPTION

*Rev* copies the named files to the standard output, reversing the order of characters in every
line. If no file is specified, the standard input is copied.

**The character here is a UTF-8 sequence, not a byte,** and that is a deliberate divergence
from v7 rather than a detail of this port. The two are the same thing on an ASCII machine; on
this one they are not. This system's text is UTF-8 throughout - the console driver, the
filesystem and the shell all carry eight bits - so `привет` is twelve bytes and six
letters, and reversing its bytes yields twelve bytes that are not text at all. *Rev* reverses
the letters:

> | **$ echo привет мир | rev**
> | рим тевирп

The rule is UTF-8's own and needs no table: a byte **10xxxxxx** is a continuation and travels
with the byte that leads it. Input that is not valid UTF-8 loses nothing - a continuation byte
with no lead comes out as a group of its own - so *rev* is safe on arbitrary bytes even though
it no longer treats them all alike.

## LONG LINES

A line longer than **1024** bytes is cut into pieces of that size and each piece is reversed on
its own, with a newline after it. That is v7's behaviour, at four times v7's buffer.

**Note:** the cut **backs off to a character boundary,** so a multi-byte letter is never split
between two pieces; the bytes not taken begin the next one. Without that the reversal above
would be a lie for exactly the lines long enough to need it.

## DIAGNOSTICS

**Note:** a final line with **no newline** is printed, with a newline added. v7 discarded
whatever was in its buffer when it saw end of file, so **printf abc | rev** produced nothing at
all.

A file that cannot be opened is reported and **ends the run,** the remaining arguments not
being read. That is v7's and is left alone. The exit status is 1.

## SEE ALSO

cat(1), tail(1)
